window.RG = window.RG || {};
var RG = window.RG;

RG.themes = (RG.themes || []).concat([
  {
    id: "trinitarian-gospel",
    title: "The Trinitarian Gospel",
    tagline: "The story does not begin with the Fall. It begins with the Father, Son and Spirit, and their plan to share their life with us.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "John 17:24", text: "Father, I desire that they also whom you have given me be with me where I am, that they may see my glory, which you have given me, for you loved me before the foundation of the world." },
      { ref: "Ephesians 1:4–5", text: "Even as he chose us in him before the foundation of the world … having predestined us for adoption as children through Jesus Christ to himself." },
      { ref: "Hebrews 12:29", text: "For our God is a consuming fire." }
    ],
    body: [
      "Many of us learned the gospel as a story in four steps: God is holy, we sinned, Jesus paid, now we must believe. Every step has truth in it. But notice where it starts. It starts with a problem. The Trinitarian gospel starts somewhere earlier and deeper: with the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit, living in a love that had no beginning, and choosing to share it.",
      "Jesus speaks of the glory the Father gave him and the love the Father had for him ‘before the foundation of the world’ (John 17:24). Paul says we were chosen ‘in him’ before the foundation of the world, for adoption (Ephesians 1:4–5). Before there was any sin to deal with, there was a purpose: that human beings would share the Son’s own place in the Father’s heart, by the Spirit. The Fall did not create God’s plan. It met a plan already in motion.",
      "C. Baxter Kruger, a theologian from Mississippi who leads a ministry called Perichoresis, has spent his life saying this in plain words. In <em>The Great Dance</em> (2000) he describes creation as the overflow of the Trinity’s joy. In <em>Jesus and the Undoing of Adam</em> (2003) he tells how the Son entered Adam’s fallen world and undid it from the inside. <em>Across All Worlds</em> (2007) and <em>The Shack Revisited</em> (2012, a theological companion to Wm. Paul Young’s novel) press the same point: in Jesus, the triune life has already reached into our darkness, and the gospel is the news of what he has made true.",
      "Kruger draws heavily on the Scottish brothers T. F. and J. B. Torrance. They taught what is called the <em>vicarious humanity of Christ</em>. Jesus did not only die in our place. He lived in our place: believing, obeying, praying and worshipping as one of us and for us. J. B. Torrance’s small book <em>Worship, Community and the Triune God of Grace</em> (1996) contrasts worship that is ‘our’ effort to reach God with worship that is our sharing, by the Spirit, in the Son’s own worship of the Father. The first is exhausting. The second is a gift.",
      "These modern voices stand on old shoulders. Irenaeus, in the second century, said the Son ‘became what we are, that he might bring us to be even what he is himself’ (<em>Against Heresies</em>, Book V, preface). Athanasius wrote in <em>On the Incarnation</em> that the Word became human so that we might be made divine, meaning that we share God’s life by grace. For them, salvation was not mainly a change in God’s attitude. It was God healing human nature by taking it into himself.",
      "Karl Barth, the great Swiss theologian, made a striking move in his doctrine of election (<em>Church Dogmatics</em> II/2). He said that Jesus Christ is both the electing God and the elected human. God’s ‘choosing’ is not first about sorting individuals into saved and lost. It is God choosing himself to be for humanity, and choosing humanity in Christ. Whether Barth was a universalist is still debated; he himself refused the label. But his work opened a door that Torrance, Kruger and many others walked through.",
      "George MacDonald, the nineteenth-century Scottish preacher and novelist, added fire and tenderness. In his <em>Unspoken Sermons</em> (three series, 1867–1889) he preached that God is a Father whose love will not stop working until his children are pure. His sermon ‘The Consuming Fire’ takes Hebrews 12:29 to mean that God’s fire is his love, which burns away only what harms us. C. S. Lewis called MacDonald his ‘master’, said that reading <em>Phantastes</em> had ‘baptised’ his imagination, and made him a guide figure in <em>The Great Divorce</em>.",
      "Put together, these voices say something simple. The gospel is Trinitarian news. The Father has always loved us in his Son. The Son has come into our broken humanity and brought it home. The Spirit is pouring that life into us so we can know it and live it. Our part is not to make it true, but to wake up to it, trust it and join in."
    ],
    separation: "The gospel begins with God’s holiness and our sin. God the Father is offended, the Son makes a payment, and the Spirit helps individuals respond. The Trinity is true but mostly operates in the background of a legal arrangement between God and each sinner.",
    union: "The gospel begins with the eternal love of Father, Son and Spirit and their purpose to share it. The Fall is a real tragedy, but it is met by a plan older than it. In Jesus the Trinity has entered our humanity, healed it and lifted it into the Father’s embrace, and the Spirit awakens us to this.",
    voices: [
      { name: "C. Baxter Kruger", note: "Founder of Perichoresis ministries; author of <em>The Great Dance</em>, <em>Jesus and the Undoing of Adam</em>, <em>Across All Worlds</em> and <em>The Shack Revisited</em>." },
      { name: "George MacDonald", note: "Scottish author of <em>Unspoken Sermons</em>; preached that God’s ‘consuming fire’ is his purifying love. C. S. Lewis called him his master." },
      { name: "T. F. Torrance", note: "Scottish theologian of the vicarious humanity of Christ and the ‘ontological’ reach of the incarnation; author of <em>The Mediation of Christ</em>." },
      { name: "J. B. Torrance", note: "Author of <em>Worship, Community and the Triune God of Grace</em> (1996), contrasting do-it-yourself worship with sharing in Christ’s worship." },
      { name: "Irenaeus and Athanasius", note: "Early church fathers who saw salvation as the Son taking our nature to heal it and bring us into God’s life." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Doesn’t starting with love rather than sin make sin seem small?", a: "It need not. Kruger and the Torrances take sin very seriously; they see it as so deep that only God himself entering our humanity could heal it. Starting with love does not shrink sin. It shows how much love was willing to do about it." },
      { q: "Isn’t this just universalism in Trinitarian clothing?", a: "Some in this stream are hopeful universalists, others are not. T. F. Torrance rejected universalism as a logical system while insisting Christ died for all. Barth refused the label. Critics in the Reformed tradition argue that if all are included, faith and the warnings of Scripture lose their force. It is a live debate, and the Trinitarian gospel does not settle it by itself." },
      { q: "Is it fair to read Irenaeus and Athanasius as supporting this modern movement?", a: "They clearly taught that the incarnation heals human nature and that salvation is sharing God’s life. They also taught judgment, the church and the need for faith and baptism. Modern Trinitarian teachers draw real threads from them, but the fathers should be read in their own setting, not as members of any modern camp." },
      { q: "Is George MacDonald really a safe guide? Wasn’t he removed from his pastorate?", a: "MacDonald left his Arundel congregation in the 1850s after objections to his views, including his hope that the heathen might be saved and his questioning of strict Calvinist teaching. He remained a devout Christian writer for the rest of his life and deeply shaped C. S. Lewis, G. K. Chesterton and others. Readers in traditional churches still disagree with his hope for universal restoration, so he should be read as a gifted but debated voice." }
    ],
    related: ["triune-dance", "trinity-restoration", "incarnation", "undoing-adam", "across-all-worlds", "inclusion"]
  },
  {
    id: "trinity-restoration",
    title: "The Trinity at Work",
    tagline: "The Father purposes, the Son takes on our humanity, the Spirit unites us to him. Restoration is the work of all three together.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Ephesians 1:9–10", text: "Making known to us the mystery of his will … to sum up all things in Christ, the things in the heavens and the things on the earth, in him." },
      { ref: "Acts 2:17", text: "‘It will be in the last days, says God, that I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh.’" },
      { ref: "Romans 8:15", text: "For you didn’t receive the spirit of bondage again to fear, but you received the Spirit of adoption, by whom we cry, ‘Abba! Father!’" },
      { ref: "Galatians 2:20", text: "I have been crucified with Christ, and it is no longer I that live, but Christ living in me." }
    ],
    body: [
      "It is easy to picture salvation as something the Son does while the Father waits to be satisfied and the Spirit tidies up afterwards. The New Testament gives a richer picture. Every part of our restoration is the shared work of Father, Son and Spirit. The old church saying was that the works of the Trinity toward the world are undivided. Each person acts in his own way, but never alone.",
      "<strong>The Father’s purpose.</strong> Ephesians 1:3–10 is one long, breathless sentence in Greek. It praises the Father who blessed us in Christ, chose us in him before the world began, destined us for adoption, lavished grace on us, and planned ‘to sum up all things in Christ’. The Greek word <em>anakephalaiōsasthai</em> means to gather up under one head. The Father’s will is not a reluctant rescue. It is a joyful plan to bring everything home to its head, which is his Son.",
      "<strong>The Son’s assumption of humanity.</strong> The Son did not visit humanity from the outside. He took our nature into himself, ‘in the likeness of sinful flesh’ (Romans 8:3), and carried it through birth, temptation, death and resurrection. Gregory of Nazianzus put the logic in a famous line: what the Son has not assumed, he has not healed (Letter 101). Because he took all of our humanity, all of it can be healed.",
      "In that humanity the Son offered what we could not. T. F. and J. B. Torrance called this his <em>vicarious</em> faith, obedience and worship. He trusted the Father for us, obeyed for us, prayed for us and still intercedes for us (Hebrews 7:25). Some translations of Galatians 2:20 even speak of living ‘by the faith of the Son of God’ (KJV). Our faith is real, but it rests on his.",
      "<strong>The Spirit poured on all flesh.</strong> At Pentecost Peter quoted Joel: God will pour out his Spirit ‘on all flesh’ (Acts 2:17). The Spirit is the one who joins us to Christ. Paul says we cry ‘Abba! Father!’ by the Spirit (Romans 8:15). Calvin himself called the Spirit the bond by which Christ unites us to himself. The Spirit takes what is true of us in Christ and makes it known, felt and lived in us.",
      "Irenaeus, writing around AD 180, called the Son and the Spirit the <em>hands of God</em> (<em>Against Heresies</em> IV.20.1; V.6.1; “two hands” is the usual summary of his image). With these two hands the Father formed Adam from the dust, and with the same two hands he is shaping humanity toward its full likeness to God. The picture is gentle and personal. God does not save us at arm’s length. He works on us with his own hands.",
      "<strong>The Father’s embrace.</strong> The end of the story is not a courtroom but a homecoming. In the parable of the lost son, the father runs, embraces, kisses, clothes and feasts (Luke 15:20–24). Jesus told that story to show what his Father is like. Restoration means being brought by the Spirit, in the Son, into that embrace.",
      "And it reaches further than individuals. Peter preached ‘the times of restoration of all things’ (Acts 3:21, Greek <em>apokatastasis</em>). Paul says creation itself will be set free (Romans 8:21) and that God was pleased to reconcile all things through Christ (Colossians 1:20). How far this restoration finally reaches is debated. That it is the shared work and joy of Father, Son and Spirit is not."
    ],
    separation: "The Father demands justice, the Son satisfies it, and the Spirit applies the benefits to those who believe. The persons can seem to have different attitudes toward us: the Father stern, the Son kind, the Spirit a helper.",
    union: "The Father, Son and Spirit share one love and one will toward us. The Father plans to gather all things in Christ, the Son takes our humanity and offers faith, obedience and worship in it, and the Spirit joins us to him and cries ‘Abba’ in us. Restoration is a family work from first to last.",
    voices: [
      { name: "Irenaeus of Lyons", note: "Second-century bishop who called the Son and Spirit the ‘two hands’ of God and taught that Christ ‘recapitulates’ humanity." },
      { name: "Gregory of Nazianzus", note: "Fourth-century theologian who argued that what Christ did not assume he did not heal." },
      { name: "John Calvin", note: "Reformer who called the Holy Spirit the bond by which Christ unites us to himself (<em>Institutes</em> III.1)." },
      { name: "T. F. and J. B. Torrance", note: "Taught that Christ offers faith, obedience and worship on our behalf, and that the Spirit lets us share in it." },
      { name: "C. Baxter Kruger", note: "Popularises the Torrances’ vision, describing the Spirit as the one who brings Jesus’ relationship with the Father into our lives." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Doesn’t Scripture speak of the Father’s wrath being turned away by the Son?", a: "Some texts use the language of propitiation (Romans 3:25, 1 John 2:2), and many traditions read them as the Son satisfying the Father’s justice. The relational view reads them through 2 Corinthians 5:19, ‘God was in Christ reconciling the world to himself’. The Father is not the problem the Son solves; the Father is in the Son, solving our problem. Both readings claim biblical support, and this is a real debate." },
      { q: "If the Spirit is poured on ‘all flesh’, why does Scripture also say some do not have the Spirit (Romans 8:9, Jude 19)?", a: "That is a fair point. Joel’s promise in Acts 2 is fulfilled for ‘everyone whom the Lord our God will call’ (Acts 2:39). Relational teachers distinguish the Spirit’s universal presence and outpouring from the Spirit’s indwelling and conscious knowing in believers. Traditional readers say the outpouring is for the church. Both affirm the Spirit is at work far beyond what we see." },
      { q: "Isn’t ‘vicarious faith’ a way of saying our own faith doesn’t matter?", a: "Not in the Torrances. They insist our faith matters deeply, but that it is a sharing in Christ’s faithfulness rather than a work that earns anything. Critics worry this can make personal response seem optional, and good teaching in this stream keeps calling people to trust." }
    ],
    related: ["trinitarian-gospel", "triune-dance", "incarnation", "apokatastasis", "union-sonship", "salvific-ontology"]
  },
  {
    id: "union-sonship",
    title: "Union and Sonship",
    tagline: "You are not a servant hoping to be promoted. You are a child, sharing the Son’s own place with the Father.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Galatians 4:6–7", text: "And because you are children, God sent out the Spirit of his Son into your hearts, crying, ‘Abba, Father!’ So you are no longer a bondservant, but a son; and if a son, then an heir of God through Christ." },
      { ref: "Romans 8:16–17", text: "The Spirit himself testifies with our spirit that we are children of God; and if children, then heirs; heirs of God, and joint heirs with Christ." },
      { ref: "1 John 4:17", text: "In this, love has been made perfect among us, that we may have boldness in the day of judgment, because as he is, even so are we in this world." },
      { ref: "Ephesians 2:6", text: "And raised us up with him, and made us to sit with him in the heavenly places in Christ Jesus." }
    ],
    body: [
      "Paul uses a special word for what God has done: <em>huiothesia</em>, often translated ‘adoption’. It literally means ‘placing as a son’. In the Roman world, adoption was not about rescuing an orphan out of pity. A grown person could be adopted to become a full heir, with a new name, a new family, and old debts cancelled. Paul uses the word five times (Romans 8:15, 8:23, 9:4; Galatians 4:5; Ephesians 1:5). Paul’s language is ‘sons’, but he clearly includes daughters (2 Corinthians 6:18). Sonship here means a place in the family, not gender.",
      "Galatians 4:4–7 tells the story in order. God sent his Son, born of a woman, born under the law, so that we might receive the place of sons. Then, ‘because you are children’, God sent the Spirit of his Son into our hearts, crying ‘Abba, Father!’ Notice the order. We are not made children because we cry out. The Spirit cries out in us because we already are children.",
      "‘Abba’ is the Aramaic word Jesus used in Gethsemane (Mark 14:36). It is warm and personal, the word a child, and also a grown son, would use for a father. It is often said to mean ‘Daddy’. That goes a little too far; scholars such as James Barr pointed out that adults used it too. But it is intimate, and the astonishing thing is that the Spirit puts Jesus’ own word for God into our mouths.",
      "This is the heart of the relational view of sonship. We are not given a second-class sonship next to Jesus. We are brought by the Spirit into <em>his</em> sonship, sharing in the Son’s own relationship with the Father. Jesus said the Father loves us even as he loves the Son (John 17:23). We are ‘joint heirs with Christ’ (Romans 8:17). John even dares to say, ‘as he is, even so are we in this world’ (1 John 4:17), and Paul says we are already seated with Christ in the heavenly places (Ephesians 2:6).",
      "Many believers live with what teachers call an <em>orphan mindset</em>. Orphans feel they must earn their place, fear being sent away, compete for love, and hide their weakness. Sons and daughters rest. They know they belong before they do anything. Paul contrasts the ‘spirit of bondage again to fear’ with ‘the Spirit of adoption’ (Romans 8:15). The difference is not how hard you try. It is whether you know whose you are.",
      "This is why identity comes before behaviour in the New Testament. Paul’s letters almost always tell us who we are in Christ first and only then how to live. Ephesians spends three chapters on what God has done before saying, ‘walk worthy of the calling’ (4:1). Good fruit grows from a secure root. Trying to behave your way into the family is like a branch trying to grow its way onto the vine.",
      "Jesus’ parable of the two sons (Luke 15:11–32) shows both ways of missing it. The younger son thinks he has lost his sonship and hopes to come back as a hired servant. The father will not even let him finish the speech. The elder son never left home, yet speaks like a slave: ‘these many years I have served you’. The father’s answer to him is just as tender: ‘Son, you are always with me, and all that is mine is yours.’ Both needed to discover they were already loved as sons.",
      "Sonship also means inheritance. Heirs do not earn an estate; they receive it because of who they are. Paul says the whole creation is waiting eagerly for the revealing of the children of God (Romans 8:19). As sons and daughters wake up to who they are, they begin to carry the Father’s heart into the world: his mercy, his healing, his peace. That is not a ladder to sonship. It is what sonship looks like when it grows up."
    ],
    separation: "We are sinners who may become children of God if we believe. Even after conversion, our standing feels fragile, and much of the Christian life is spent trying to prove ourselves faithful servants, hoping to hear ‘well done’ in the end.",
    union: "We have been placed as sons and daughters in Christ, sharing his own relationship with the Father. The Spirit cries ‘Abba’ in us because we are children. We live from belonging, not toward it, and our behaviour grows out of a secure identity.",
    voices: [
      { name: "The Apostle Paul", note: "The only New Testament writer to use <em>huiothesia</em>, ‘placing as a son’, for God’s act of adoption." },
      { name: "C. Baxter Kruger", note: "Teaches that the Spirit brings us to share Jesus’ own ‘Abba’ relationship, not a separate copy of it." },
      { name: "J. I. Packer", note: "Evangelical theologian who, in <em>Knowing God</em> (1973), called adoption the highest privilege the gospel offers. A traditional voice who shares the emphasis." },
      { name: "Henri Nouwen", note: "Catholic priest whose <em>The Return of the Prodigal Son</em> (1992) explores becoming the beloved son and, finally, the father." },
      { name: "Jack Frost", note: "Founder of Shiloh Place Ministries, widely associated with teaching on the ‘orphan heart’ versus the ‘heart of sonship’." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Doesn’t John 1:12 say we become children of God only when we receive him?", a: "Yes: ‘as many as received him, to them he gave the right to become God’s children’. Traditional theology reads this as adoption happening at conversion. Relational teachers distinguish humanity’s objective inclusion in Christ from the personal receiving and knowing of it. Both agree that receiving matters; they differ on whether faith creates the relationship or awakens us to it." },
      { q: "Isn’t ‘as he is, so are we’ an overstatement? We still sin.", a: "John is honest that we sin (1 John 1:8). In context, 4:17 is about love and boldness before God: our standing before the Father is the same as Jesus’ standing. It is not a claim that we are morally perfect now, but a claim about where we stand." },
      { q: "Can talk of sonship make people careless about obedience?", a: "It can if misused. But in Scripture sonship is the reason for obedience, not its replacement. Children of a loving Father want to be like him (Ephesians 5:1). Fear-based obedience is fragile; love-based obedience lasts." },
      { q: "Is the ‘orphan versus son’ language actually in the Bible?", a: "The phrase ‘orphan spirit’ is modern ministry language, not a biblical term. But the contrast it describes is biblical: Jesus promised, ‘I will not leave you orphans’ (John 14:18, KJV ‘comfortless’, Greek <em>orphanous</em>), and Paul contrasts the spirit of bondage and fear with the Spirit of adoption (Romans 8:15). The language is a helpful summary as long as it is not treated as Scripture itself." }
    ],
    related: ["separation-union", "trinity-restoration", "manifest-sons", "inclusion", "designed-for-connection", "salvific-ontology"]
  },
  {
    id: "designed-for-connection",
    title: "Designed for Connection",
    tagline: "God said ‘let us’ and made us in his image. We were made by a relationship, for relationship.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Genesis 1:26–27", text: "God said, ‘Let’s make man in our image, after our likeness’ … God created man in his own image. In God’s image he created him; male and female he created them." },
      { ref: "Genesis 2:18", text: "Yahweh God said, ‘It is not good for the man to be alone. I will make him a helper comparable to him.’" },
      { ref: "Genesis 3:10", text: "‘I heard your voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself.’" },
      { ref: "1 Corinthians 13:12", text: "For now we see in a mirror, dimly, but then face to face. Now I know in part, but then I will know fully, even as I was also fully known." }
    ],
    body: [
      "The first thing the Bible says about human beings is that God made them in his image (Genesis 1:26–27). Christians have argued for centuries about what the image is. Some said reason. Some said the soul. Some said our role as God’s representatives ruling creation, which is how many Old Testament scholars read it today. Relational theologians add another layer: the God who says ‘let us’ makes humans ‘male and female’. The image is seen not only in each person but in persons together.",
      "Christians have long read the ‘us’ as a hint of the Trinity. Jewish interpreters and many Old Testament scholars read it differently, as God speaking with his heavenly court or as a plural of majesty. So it is honest to call the Trinitarian reading a Christian rereading, not the text’s only possible sense. Yet the deeper point holds either way. The God of the Bible is not lonely, and the humans he makes are not meant to be either.",
      "Before the Fall, before any sin, God looks at the man alone and says, ‘It is not good’ (Genesis 2:18). It is the first ‘not good’ in the Bible. Aloneness was a problem in paradise. We were made to be known and to know, to love and be loved. Karl Barth read the image of God as this being-in-relationship, ‘I and thou’, reflecting God’s own life. Many today see loneliness as one of the deepest poverties a person can suffer.",
      "Genesis 3 shows what happens when trust breaks. The first thing Adam and Eve do is cover themselves. The first thing they say to God is, ‘I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself’ (3:10). Shame makes us hide from God and from each other. The gospel story runs in the other direction: from hiding to being found, from fig leaves to being clothed by God, and finally to knowing ‘even as I was also fully known’ (1 Corinthians 13:12).",
      "Modern science offers some interesting echoes, and they should be handled modestly. Attachment research, beginning with John Bowlby and Mary Ainsworth, shows how deeply children are shaped by secure or insecure bonds with caregivers. The field called interpersonal neurobiology, associated with the psychiatrist Daniel Siegel, explores how relationships shape the developing brain and mind. The Christian psychiatrist Curt Thompson, in <em>Anatomy of the Soul</em> (2010) and <em>The Soul of Shame</em> (2015), draws on this work to argue that we are ‘known into being’ and that shame is healed in safe, loving community. Science does not prove theology, and popular claims about the brain often run ahead of the research. But it is striking that careful study keeps finding that humans flourish when they are safely known.",
      "This is why Jesus’ ministry was so full of tables. He ate with tax collectors and sinners (Mark 2:15–17), was called ‘a friend of tax collectors and sinners’ (Luke 7:34), fed crowds, cooked breakfast on a beach for friends who had failed him (John 21:9–13), and gave his church a meal to remember him by. In the ancient world, to share a table was to share life. Jesus’ table was a living picture of God’s welcome.",
      "It is also why the whole law is fulfilled in love. Paul says, ‘love doesn’t harm a neighbour. Love therefore is the fulfilment of the law’ (Romans 13:10). Jesus summed up everything in loving God and loving neighbour (Matthew 22:37–40). Commandments are not the goal. Connection is. The rules were always signposts toward right relationship.",
      "So the church is meant to be a family, not an audience. The New Testament calls believers brothers and sisters hundreds of times and calls the church ‘the household of God’ (Ephesians 2:19). If we are made for communion like the God who made us, then salvation is not only forgiveness for individuals. It is being brought home into the fellowship of the Father, Son and Spirit, and into the family of all his children."
    ],
    separation: "Humans are individual souls who need to get right with God one at a time. Relationships with others are important but secondary; the main thing is a private transaction between me and God. Emotions and needs for connection are often treated as weakness or distraction.",
    union: "Humans are made in the image of a relational God, for communion with him and with each other. Shame and hiding are what sin does; being fully known and loved is what grace restores. The church, the table and friendship are not extras but the shape of salvation itself.",
    voices: [
      { name: "Karl Barth", note: "Read the image of God as relational, seen in humanity as ‘male and female’, reflecting the fellowship within God." },
      { name: "Curt Thompson", note: "Christian psychiatrist, author of <em>Anatomy of the Soul</em> (2010) and <em>The Soul of Shame</em> (2015), bringing interpersonal neurobiology into dialogue with faith." },
      { name: "Daniel Siegel", note: "Psychiatrist associated with interpersonal neurobiology, which studies how relationships shape the mind and brain. Not a theological writer." },
      { name: "Dietrich Bonhoeffer", note: "German pastor whose <em>Life Together</em> (1939) describes Christian community as a gift of grace, not an ideal we create." },
      { name: "John Zizioulas", note: "Orthodox theologian whose <em>Being as Communion</em> (1985) argues that personhood is fundamentally relational. His reading of the fathers is debated." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Isn’t the ‘let us’ of Genesis 1:26 really about angels or a royal plural?", a: "Those are common readings among Jewish and many Christian scholars. The Trinitarian reading developed later, in light of Christ, and should be presented as a Christian rereading. The relational point does not depend on it, since Genesis 2:18 and the whole biblical story show humans made for relationship." },
      { q: "Isn’t bringing neuroscience into theology risky?", a: "Yes, if we overstate it. Brain science is complex and changing, and popular summaries often oversimplify. It is best used as an echo, not a proof: research on attachment and relationships fits well with what Scripture says, but faith does not rest on it." },
      { q: "Doesn’t the Bible also value solitude?", a: "It does. Jesus withdrew to pray alone (Luke 5:16), and the desert tradition prized silence. But solitude in Scripture is time alone <em>with God</em>, which then sends us back to people. It is never meant to become isolation." }
    ],
    related: ["triune-dance", "separation-union", "church-now", "union-sonship", "sacred-secular", "sin"]
  }
  ,
  {
    id: "church-now",
    title: "The Church Now",
    tagline: "The church is not a building, a gatekeeper or a show. It is a family that makes the Father visible.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Matthew 5:14, 16", text: "You are the light of the world. A city located on a hill can’t be hidden … Even so, let your light shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father who is in heaven." },
      { ref: "1 Peter 2:9", text: "But you are a chosen race, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, a people for God’s own possession, that you may proclaim the excellence of him who called you out of darkness into his marvellous light." },
      { ref: "2 Corinthians 5:19–20", text: "God was in Christ reconciling the world to himself, not reckoning to them their trespasses, and having committed to us the word of reconciliation. We are therefore ambassadors on behalf of Christ." },
      { ref: "Ephesians 4:15–16", text: "Speaking truth in love, we may grow up in all things into him who is the head, Christ, from whom all the body … makes the body increase to the building up of itself in love." }
    ],
    body: [
      "The New Testament word for church is <em>ekklēsia</em>. In Greek cities it meant the assembly of citizens called out of their homes to gather and govern the city’s life. The Greek Old Testament (the Septuagint) also used it for the ‘assembly’ of Israel gathered before God. So the word carries both ideas: a people called together by God, and a people with a public role in the world. It never meant a building. The English word ‘church’ comes from a different Greek word, <em>kyriakon</em>, ‘belonging to the Lord’.",
      "Paul’s favourite picture is the <em>body of Christ</em> (1 Corinthians 12; Romans 12; Ephesians 4). A body is not a club with members who sign up. It is one life shared by many parts, each needed, none superior. The hand cannot say to the foot, ‘I have no need of you’. The weaker parts are treated with special honour (1 Corinthians 12:21–23). In a body, the life comes from the head, Christ, and flows through every part.",
      "Another picture is the <em>household</em> or family of God (Ephesians 2:19; 1 Timothy 3:15). Early Christians met mostly in homes, ate together, shared possessions and cared for widows and orphans (Acts 2:42–47; James 1:27). The church was a family where slaves and free, Jews and Gentiles, men and women sat at one table (Galatians 3:28). That was a scandal in the ancient world, and it was the point.",
      "What is this family for? Jesus said his followers are ‘the light of the world’, so that people would see their good works ‘and glorify your Father’ (Matthew 5:14–16). The church exists to make the Father visible. Peter says the whole people of God are ‘a royal priesthood’ (1 Peter 2:9), which is the root of the Reformation teaching of the priesthood of all believers. Every believer stands before God and carries God to others. And Paul says God has given us ‘the ministry of reconciliation’ (2 Corinthians 5:18–20): announcing that in Christ God has reconciled the world to himself.",
      "The church gathers around word, table and water. Baptism and the Lord’s Supper are not hoops to jump through. In the relational view they are enacted announcements of what is already true in Christ: we died and rose with him (Romans 6:3–4), and we share one bread because we are one body (1 Corinthians 10:17). Christians differ on what happens in the sacraments, but all agree they are gifts that gather us into Christ’s life.",
      "Gifts are given ‘for the perfecting of the saints, to the work of serving, to the building up of the body of Christ’ (Ephesians 4:12). Leaders are not a separate class who do the ministry while everyone watches. They equip everyone to do it. And the church has always been called to the poor. Jesus’ first sermon announced good news to the poor (Luke 4:18); the early church made sure ‘there was not anyone among them who lacked’ (Acts 4:34).",
      "So what is the church <em>not</em> for, in a union gospel? It is not the gatekeeper that decides who God may love, because Christ has already torn the veil (Matthew 27:51). It is not a mediator standing between people and God, because ‘there is one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus’ (1 Timothy 2:5). It is not mainly a sin-management system, keeping people in line through fear. And it is not a performance venue where a few perform and the many consume. It is a family gathered around the Father’s table.",
      "There is an honest counterpoint, and it matters. Saying the church is not a gatekeeper does not mean anything goes. The New Testament is full of teaching, correction and mutual care: ‘exhort one another day by day’ (Hebrews 3:13), ‘bear one another’s burdens’ (Galatians 6:2), ‘don’t forsake our own assembling together’ (Hebrews 10:25). Healthy families have rhythms, boundaries and truth-telling. A union gospel does not abolish accountability. It roots it in love rather than fear, and aims it at healing rather than exclusion."
    ],
    separation: "The church is the place where saved people gather, protected from the world. It controls access to God through right belief, sacraments and membership, manages sin through rules and discipline, and depends on trained professionals while most members attend.",
    union: "The church is the visible family of a Father who has reconciled the world in Christ. It gathers around word, table and water, equips every member as a priest, carries the ministry of reconciliation outward, and cares for the poor. Accountability and teaching remain, but as the care of a family, not the guarding of a gate.",
    voices: [
      { name: "Martin Luther", note: "Reformer who championed the priesthood of all believers, drawing on 1 Peter 2:9, against a sharp clergy–laity divide." },
      { name: "Dietrich Bonhoeffer", note: "Wrote <em>Life Together</em> (1939) on Christian community, and spoke of the church as ‘Christ existing as community’." },
      { name: "Lesslie Newbigin", note: "Missionary bishop who called the local congregation the ‘hermeneutic of the gospel’, the way people understand the good news." },
      { name: "Wm. Paul Young", note: "Author of <em>The Shack</em>, which portrays relationship with God as more central than religious institutions, a theme that drew both praise and criticism." },
      { name: "Brian Zahnd", note: "Pastor of Word of Life Church in St. Joseph, Missouri, who moved from a growth-focused model to a more liturgical, Christ-centred community." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Doesn’t Jesus give the church authority to ‘bind and loose’ and even to exclude (Matthew 18:15–18)?", a: "He does, and Paul practised church discipline (1 Corinthians 5). The relational view reads these texts as aiming at restoration. Paul’s goal was ‘that the spirit may be saved’ (5:5), and he later urged forgiveness and comfort for the repentant (2 Corinthians 2:7). Discipline is a family protecting its members, not a gate deciding God’s love." },
      { q: "If everyone is already included in Christ, why does the church matter at all?", a: "Because inclusion is meant to be known and lived. The church is where people awaken to their belonging, are taught, healed, fed and sent. Traditional Christians answer that the church matters because it is the community of the saved. Both see it as essential, for different reasons." },
      { q: "Isn’t criticising ‘performance’ churches unfair to sincere leaders?", a: "It can be. Many large churches serve people faithfully, and good music and preaching are gifts. The critique is not of excellence but of a model where most people only watch. Any church, large or small, can fall into that." }
    ],
    related: ["designed-for-connection", "sacred-secular", "inclusion", "separation-union", "undiluted-gospel", "union-sonship"]
  },
  {
    id: "undiluted-gospel",
    title: "The Undiluted Gospel",
    tagline: "The gospel is not good advice or a conditional offer. It is good news of what God has done, of great joy for all people.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Luke 2:10", text: "The angel said to them, ‘Don’t be afraid, for behold, I bring you good news of great joy which will be to all the people.’" },
      { ref: "1 Corinthians 15:3–4", text: "For I delivered to you first of all that which I also received: that Christ died for our sins according to the Scriptures, that he was buried, that he was raised on the third day according to the Scriptures." },
      { ref: "Colossians 1:19–20", text: "For all the fullness was pleased to dwell in him, and through him to reconcile all things to himself, having made peace through the blood of his cross." },
      { ref: "Galatians 1:6–7", text: "I marvel that you are so quickly deserting him who called you in the grace of Christ to a different ‘good news’, but there isn’t another ‘good news’." }
    ],
    body: [
      "The word ‘gospel’ translates the Greek <em>euangelion</em>, ‘good news’. In the Roman world it was used for royal announcements: a victory won, an heir born, a new ruler crowned. You did not make a victory happen by believing the herald. You heard that it had happened, and your life changed in response. The New Testament uses the word the same way. The gospel is first of all an announcement.",
      "Listen to what the apostles actually announced. Paul’s summary is short: Christ died for our sins, was buried, and was raised on the third day (1 Corinthians 15:3–4). Peter at Pentecost proclaimed that God had raised the crucified Jesus and made him Lord and Christ (Acts 2:22–36). Paul tells the Colossians that God was pleased through Christ ‘to reconcile all things to himself’ and that he ‘has reconciled’ them (Colossians 1:19–22). The centre of the message is not what we must do, but what God has done in Jesus.",
      "The angels at Bethlehem called it ‘good news of great joy which will be to all the people’ (Luke 2:10). The relational gospel takes that phrase seriously. If it is news of great joy for all, it cannot be news that is mostly bad for most people. It must be news about God’s faithfulness that is true for everyone before anyone responds, even if not everyone yet knows it or trusts it.",
      "Of course the apostles called people to respond: ‘Repent, and be baptised’ (Acts 2:38). Response is not optional. The question is what comes first. In the undiluted gospel, response is the fitting answer to news. In a diluted gospel, response becomes the condition that makes the news true. That small change in order changes everything. It turns good news into a contract.",
      "How does the gospel get diluted? The first case is in the New Testament itself. In Galatia, teachers said that faith in Christ was good but Gentiles also needed circumcision and law-keeping to be fully accepted. Paul called this ‘a different good news’, which is no good news at all (Galatians 1:6–9). Mixing law with grace does not add to grace; it cancels it (Galatians 5:4).",
      "Later history saw other mixtures. In the medieval West, systems of merit, penance and indulgences could leave ordinary people anxious about whether they had done enough; Luther’s protest began there. Some Protestant preaching later narrowed the gospel to a legal transaction alone, so that salvation became only a change in legal status rather than union with Christ and new life. Some revival preaching leaned heavily on fear of hell to produce decisions. And in recent times, the prosperity gospel has tied God’s favour to giving and positive confession. Each of these keeps some truth but loses the simplicity of grace.",
      "Recovering the undiluted gospel means going back to the announcement. It means starting with who God is in Jesus, telling what the Father, Son and Spirit have done, and then inviting people to believe, turn and live from it. It means preaching grace without the small print. And it means letting the gospel be good news for the poor, the sick, the shamed and the outsider first, as Jesus did (Luke 4:18).",
      "Honesty requires one more word. Every tradition believes its gospel is the undiluted one. Reformed Christians would say that the full gospel includes God’s holy wrath against sin and the necessity of personal faith, and that dropping these is the real dilution. Catholic and Orthodox Christians would say the gospel cannot be separated from the church and its sacraments. The relational view answers that wrath, faith and church are real, but must be understood through the cross as revelations of love. Readers should weigh these claims with Scripture in hand."
    ],
    separation: "The gospel is an offer: God has made salvation possible, and it becomes yours if you meet the condition of faith, repentance or obedience. Without your response, the news is bad news, because you remain under wrath.",
    union: "The gospel is an announcement: in Jesus, God has reconciled the world to himself, defeated death and brought humanity home. Faith and repentance are the joyful response to good news already true, not the price of making it true.",
    voices: [
      { name: "The Apostle Paul", note: "Defended the gospel of grace against a law-mixed message in Galatians, calling it ‘a different good news’." },
      { name: "Martin Luther", note: "Rediscovered justification by grace through faith against a system of merit and indulgences, sparking the Reformation in 1517." },
      { name: "N. T. Wright", note: "Anglican scholar who stresses that ‘gospel’ in the New Testament is royal announcement that Jesus is Lord. Not part of this stream, but an influence on how many read the word." },
      { name: "Steve McVey", note: "Founder of Grace Walk Ministries, who teaches that the gospel is the finished work of Christ, not a performance system." },
      { name: "Francois du Toit", note: "South African author of <em>The Mirror Bible</em>, a paraphrase emphasising what the gospel reveals about humanity already included in Christ." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Doesn’t the gospel include a call to repent and believe? Isn’t calling it ‘only an announcement’ unbiblical?", a: "Yes, the call is part of the gospel, and the relational view should never drop it. The point is about order and nature. The call is ‘believe the good news’ (Mark 1:15), which means the news is already good before you believe it. Traditional theologians reply that the announcement includes a warning for those who refuse, and that is a fair point to weigh." },
      { q: "Isn’t calling other people’s gospel ‘diluted’ arrogant?", a: "It can be. Paul’s strong words in Galatians were aimed at those adding law to grace, not at everyone who disagreed on secondary matters. Humility means admitting that every tradition, including this one, can mix human ideas into the message." },
      { q: "Doesn’t ‘good news for all’ just mean ‘good news offered to all’?", a: "That is how many traditional readers take it. The relational view argues that texts like 2 Corinthians 5:19 and Colossians 1:20 describe something God has already done for all. This is one of the main points of disagreement." },
      { q: "Aren’t Paul’s warnings in Galatians about losing grace a reason for fear?", a: "Paul warns that seeking to be justified by the law means ‘you are fallen away from grace’ (Galatians 5:4). Traditional readers take this as a real danger to salvation. Relational readers take it as falling out of the <em>experience</em> of grace into the bondage of performance. Either way, Paul’s cure is the same: return to the gospel of grace, not try harder at the law." }
    ],
    related: ["cross", "inclusion", "law-purpose", "separation-union", "trinitarian-gospel", "church-now"]
  },
  {
    id: "bible-narratives",
    title: "Reading the Bible’s Story",
    tagline: "The Bible is one great story, and every road in it leads to Jesus.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Luke 24:27", text: "Beginning from Moses and from all the prophets, he explained to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning himself." },
      { ref: "John 5:39", text: "You search the Scriptures, because you think that in them you have eternal life; and these are they which testify about me." },
      { ref: "1 Corinthians 10:11", text: "Now all these things happened to them by way of example, and they were written for our admonition, on whom the ends of the ages have come." },
      { ref: "Romans 5:14", text: "Nevertheless death reigned from Adam until Moses, even over those whose sins weren’t like Adam’s disobedience, who is a foreshadowing of him who was to come." }
    ],
    body: [
      "On the road to Emmaus, the risen Jesus walked with two discouraged disciples and, ‘beginning from Moses and from all the prophets’, explained the things concerning himself in all the Scriptures (Luke 24:27). Later he told the gathered disciples that everything written about him in the law, the prophets and the psalms must be fulfilled (24:44). This is the key to reading the Bible. It is not a collection of separate moral lessons. It is one story, and Jesus is its centre.",
      "The story has a shape. <strong>Creation:</strong> God makes a good world and humans in his image. <strong>Fall:</strong> humans break trust, and death, shame and violence spread. <strong>Israel:</strong> God calls one family, Abraham’s, through whom all families of the earth will be blessed (Genesis 12:3). <strong>Christ:</strong> Israel’s story comes to a head in Jesus, the faithful Israelite and the true human. <strong>New creation:</strong> through his death and resurrection God begins to make all things new (Revelation 21:5). Every part of the Bible sits somewhere on this arc.",
      "Bible stories are mostly <em>descriptive</em>, not <em>prescriptive</em>. They tell us what happened, often without comment. When Abraham lies about his wife, when Jacob tricks his brother, when Jephthah makes a terrible vow, the text is not telling us to do the same. It is showing honestly what human beings are like and how God keeps working through them. Reading every story as a model to copy leads to strange and sometimes harmful conclusions.",
      "That honesty extends to the heroes. Noah gets drunk. Moses kills a man. David commits adultery and arranges a murder. Peter denies Jesus. The Bible does not airbrush them. Hebrews 11 lists these flawed people as examples of faith, not of perfection. The real hero of every story is God, who stays faithful when people do not. Reading the characters honestly protects us from despair about our own failures and from hero-worship of leaders.",
      "The New Testament writers read the Old Testament through <em>types</em>: real people and events that foreshadow Christ. Paul calls Adam ‘a foreshadowing of him who was to come’ (Romans 5:14, Greek <em>typos</em>). Peter sees Noah’s ark as a picture of baptism (1 Peter 3:20–21). Hebrews sees Abraham receiving Isaac back ‘as a figure’ of resurrection (Hebrews 11:19). Christians have seen Joseph, rejected by his brothers and then their saviour, as a picture of Christ, though the New Testament does not say so directly. Christ is ‘our Passover’ lamb (1 Corinthians 5:7), the true bread from heaven foreshadowed by the manna (John 6:32–35), the rock that followed Israel (1 Corinthians 10:4), the one lifted up like the bronze serpent (John 3:14), the sign of Jonah (Matthew 12:40), and the Son of David whose kingdom has no end (Luke 1:32–33).",
      "The early church went further. Origen of Alexandria, in <em>On First Principles</em> (Book IV), taught that Scripture has a body, a soul and a spirit: a literal sense, a moral sense, and a spiritual sense that leads to Christ. Medieval teachers developed four senses: the <em>literal</em> (what happened), the <em>allegorical</em> (what it shows about Christ and the church), the <em>moral</em> or tropological (how we should live), and the <em>anagogical</em> (what it says about our final hope). John Cassian’s classic example was Jerusalem: a city, the church, the soul, and the heavenly city.",
      "Allegory can be beautiful and it can also be wild. The Reformers pushed back against fanciful readings and insisted on the plain sense. Many modern scholars agree that the original meaning must come first. The relational stream generally keeps both: read the text honestly in its own setting, and then read it, as the apostles did, in the light of Christ.",
      "Finally, Israel’s story is humanity’s story. Israel’s exile from the land echoes Adam’s exile from the garden. Israel’s struggle to trust God is our struggle. When Jesus comes, he lives Israel’s story faithfully: through the waters, into the wilderness for forty days, up the mountain to teach, and finally into death and out again. In him the story of Israel, and of all humanity, finds its true ending."
    ],
    separation: "The Bible is mainly a rulebook and a set of moral examples. Each story teaches a lesson to copy or avoid, and every portrait of God carries equal weight, even where it seems to conflict with Jesus.",
    union: "The Bible is one story that finds its centre and meaning in Jesus. Stories are read honestly as descriptions, heroes are seen as flawed people held by a faithful God, and the Old Testament is read through Christ as the apostles read it.",
    voices: [
      { name: "Origen of Alexandria", note: "Third-century scholar who taught that Scripture has literal, moral and spiritual senses, in <em>On First Principles</em> Book IV." },
      { name: "John Cassian", note: "Fifth-century monk who gave the classic example of the four senses using Jerusalem." },
      { name: "Augustine of Hippo", note: "Taught that the New is hidden in the Old and the Old is revealed in the New, and that all Scripture aims at love of God and neighbour." },
      { name: "Peter Enns", note: "Biblical scholar who stresses reading the Bible honestly as an ancient story, as in <em>The Bible Tells Me So</em> (2014). Not part of this stream, but often read in it." },
      { name: "Greg Boyd", note: "Proposes a cross-centred reading of the Old Testament in <em>The Crucifixion of the Warrior God</em> (2017)." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Doesn’t finding Christ everywhere risk reading things into the text?", a: "It can. Responsible Christ-centred reading follows the patterns the New Testament itself sets and respects the text’s original meaning first. Not every detail is a hidden picture of Jesus. The claim is that the whole story moves toward him, not that every verse is a code." },
      { q: "If narratives are descriptive, how do we get any guidance from them?", a: "Stories teach by showing, not by commanding. We learn what trust, failure, mercy and faithfulness look like. Paul says these things were written ‘by way of example’ (1 Corinthians 10:11). We draw lessons, but we test them by the clear teaching of Christ." },
      { q: "Doesn’t Christ-centred reading let us ignore hard passages about God’s judgment?", a: "It should not. A Christ-centred reading must face those passages and ask how they relate to the cross. Some conclude they show God accommodating his people’s limited view; others, more traditionally, that they show real judgment that Christ also bore. Both approaches take the texts seriously." }
    ],
    related: ["perfect-theology", "nature-of-god", "law-purpose", "undoing-adam", "undiluted-gospel", "cross"]
  }
  ,
  {
    id: "mystical-union",
    title: "Mystical Union",
    tagline: "Experiencing God is not a ladder we climb to reach him. It is the overflow of a union he has already made.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Acts 2:13, 15", text: "Others, mocking, said, ‘They are filled with new wine.’ … ‘For these aren’t drunken, as you suppose, seeing it is only the third hour of the day.’" },
      { ref: "Ephesians 5:18", text: "Don’t be drunken with wine, in which is dissipation, but be filled with the Spirit." },
      { ref: "Romans 14:17", text: "For God’s Kingdom is not eating and drinking, but righteousness, peace, and joy in the Holy Spirit." },
      { ref: "1 Corinthians 6:17", text: "But he who is joined to the Lord is one spirit." }
    ],
    body: [
      "The word ‘mystical’ can sound strange or spooky. In Christian history it simply means the direct, lived knowing of God, as opposed to knowing <em>about</em> him. Paul says, ‘he who is joined to the Lord is one spirit’ (1 Corinthians 6:17), and speaks of ‘Christ in you, the hope of glory’ as a mystery now revealed (Colossians 1:27). Christian mysticism is the long tradition of people who took those words as an invitation to experience, not just a doctrine.",
      "That tradition is deep and wide. The <strong>Desert Fathers and Mothers</strong> of fourth-century Egypt went into the wilderness to pray and found God in silence. <strong>Gregory of Nyssa</strong>, in <em>The Life of Moses</em>, described the soul’s ascent like Moses climbing Sinai into the ‘thick darkness where God was’ (Exodus 20:21): the closer we come, the more God exceeds our ideas, and the journey into him never ends. <strong>Bernard of Clairvaux</strong> preached his <em>Sermons on the Song of Songs</em> as a love story between Christ and the soul. <strong>Julian of Norwich</strong> received visions of Christ’s love and heard that ‘all shall be well’.",
      "Later voices added more. <strong>Teresa of Avila</strong>, in <em>The Interior Castle</em> (1577), pictured the soul as a castle of many rooms with God dwelling at the centre. <strong>John of the Cross</strong> wrote of the ‘dark night’, a season where God seems absent but is actually purifying and drawing the soul deeper. <strong>Brother Lawrence</strong>, a Carmelite kitchen worker, became known through <em>The Practice of the Presence of God</em> for finding God as truly among the pots and pans as in the chapel. These writers speak of rest, of loving attention, of joy, and sometimes of ecstasy.",
      "Scripture itself contains strong experiences of the Spirit. At Pentecost, the disciples were so overwhelmed that onlookers said, ‘They are filled with new wine’. Peter replied that they were not drunk, since it was only nine in the morning (Acts 2:13–15). Paul contrasts being drunk with wine with being ‘filled with the Spirit’ (Ephesians 5:18), and the comparison suggests that the Spirit, like wine, affects the whole person. Paul also says the kingdom is ‘righteousness, peace, and joy in the Holy Spirit’ (Romans 14:17). Joy and rest are not decorations of the faith; they are part of its substance.",
      "<strong>John Crowder</strong> is a contemporary voice who has tried to bring this tradition into charismatic and grace circles. He founded Sons of Thunder Ministries and Publications and has written books including <em>The New Mystics</em> (2006), <em>Mystical Union</em> (2010), <em>Cosmos Reborn</em>, <em>Seven Spirits Burning</em>, <em>The Ecstasy of Loving God</em> and <em>Chosen for Paradise</em>. His themes are humanity’s union with God in Christ, the finished work of the cross, grace, and ecstatic joy, which he describes with phrases like ‘drunken glory’ and being ‘drunk in the Spirit’, drawing on Ephesians 5:18 and Acts 2.",
      "Crowder draws on the older mystics, including Teresa of Avila, John of the Cross, Bernard of Clairvaux and the Desert Fathers, and on stories of saints’ ecstasies. The kinds of experiences he and the tradition describe include ecstasy, holy laughter, trances, visions and deep rest. Christians have reported such things in many revivals, and also in the lives of the saints.",
      "His distinctive claim is about <em>where experience comes from</em>. Many mystical writers described stages or ladders: purgation, illumination, then union, reached by long discipline. Crowder turns this around. Union is not the top of the ladder. It is the starting point, already accomplished in Christ’s incarnation, death and resurrection. Experience is not how we reach God, but the fruit of being already joined to him. He often puts it this way: the mystical experience is Jesus himself. Our part is not to climb but to enjoy.",
      "This matters for ordinary believers. If experience is a ladder, those who feel nothing may think they have failed, and those who feel a lot may become proud. If experience is the overflow of union, then the quiet believer and the ecstatic one stand on the same ground: Christ in them. Feelings come and go, as John of the Cross knew well. Union does not."
    ],
    separation: "God is far off and must be approached through effort, discipline and purification. Mystical experience is a reward for the advanced few, reached by climbing a ladder of holiness, and strong emotional experiences are suspect.",
    union: "We are already joined to God in Christ. Prayer, contemplation and even ecstatic joy are ways of awakening to and enjoying that union, not ways of earning it. Experiences are welcome as fruit, but union does not depend on them.",
    voices: [
      { name: "Gregory of Nyssa", note: "Fourth-century bishop whose <em>The Life of Moses</em> describes an endless ascent into God through ‘luminous darkness’." },
      { name: "Teresa of Avila", note: "Spanish Carmelite reformer, author of <em>The Interior Castle</em> (1577), picturing God dwelling at the soul’s centre." },
      { name: "John of the Cross", note: "Spanish mystic and poet who wrote of the ‘dark night of the soul’ as God’s hidden work of purifying love." },
      { name: "Brother Lawrence", note: "Seventeenth-century Carmelite lay brother known for <em>The Practice of the Presence of God</em>, finding God in ordinary work." },
      { name: "John Crowder", note: "Founder of Sons of Thunder; author of <em>The New Mystics</em> and <em>Mystical Union</em>, presenting mystical experience as the fruit of finished union in Christ." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Isn’t ‘drunk in the Spirit’ a misuse of Ephesians 5:18? Paul is contrasting the Spirit with drunkenness, not recommending it.", a: "Many commentators say exactly this: Paul sets the Spirit-filled life against drunkenness, and the fruit he describes next is singing, thanksgiving and mutual submission (5:19–21). Crowder and others reply that the comparison only works because both affect the whole person, and Acts 2 shows onlookers mistaking the Spirit’s effects for drunkenness. Readers should weigh both, and note that Paul’s emphasis is on worship and love, not spectacle." },
      { q: "Critics call this ‘hyper-grace’. Is that fair?", a: "Reformed critics, for example writers published by Monergism, have described Crowder’s teaching as hyper-grace and charismatic excess, arguing that it downplays repentance, holiness and sober-mindedness (1 Peter 5:8). Those in his stream reply that they preach holiness as the fruit of union, not as a condition for it. This is a real disagreement about emphasis and should be stated fairly." },
      { q: "Weren’t the classical mystics very different from modern charismatic ecstasy?", a: "Often, yes. Teresa and John of the Cross were cautious about visions and raptures and warned against seeking them. They stressed humility, love and the virtues as the true signs of God’s work. Appealing to them for modern ecstatic practice is partly fair, since they did report such experiences, and partly a stretch, since they valued quiet faithfulness more." },
      { q: "How can we tell a genuine experience of God from emotional excitement or manipulation?", a: "The tradition is very practical here. Jesus said a tree is known by its fruit (Matthew 7:16), and Paul lists the fruit of the Spirit: love, joy, peace, patience, kindness and the rest (Galatians 5:22–23). Teresa of Avila judged experiences by whether they produced humility and love. Any stream, whether quiet or ecstatic, can be tested by whether it makes people more like Jesus." }
    ],
    related: ["triune-dance", "separation-union", "sacred-secular", "union-sonship", "trinitarian-gospel", "sacred-biology"]
  },
  {
    id: "sacred-biology",
    title: "The Body in New Creation",
    tagline: "The body is not a souvenir of Adam to escape from. It is God’s chosen instrument and a sanctuary for Christ.",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Genesis 2:7", text: "Yahweh God formed man from the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul." },
      { ref: "John 1:14", text: "The Word became flesh, and lived among us." },
      { ref: "Romans 8:11", text: "But if the Spirit of him who raised up Jesus from the dead dwells in you, he who raised up Christ Jesus from the dead will also give life to your mortal bodies through his Spirit who dwells in you." },
      { ref: "1 Corinthians 6:19–20", text: "Or don’t you know that your body is a temple of the Holy Spirit which is in you, which you have from God? … Therefore glorify God in your body and in your spirit, which are God’s." }
    ],
    body: [
      "This theme draws on a recent book on embodied New Creation theology (2026), written by a contemporary New Creation teacher with two co-authors. It presents the body as God’s chosen instrument for healing, wholeness and the kingdom now. According to the publisher’s description, the book combines New Creation theology, nervous-system science and embodied transformation, drawing on Scripture and the early church fathers.",
      "The book challenges a gospel that, in its words, ‘rescues the soul while leaving the body behind’. That gospel treats sickness as a teacher, death as a promotion, and heaven as an escape. The book argues instead that God’s plan has always moved through bodies. God formed the first human from the dust and breathed into him (Genesis 2:7). God entered a body in the Incarnation: ‘the Word became flesh’ (John 1:14). God redeems bodies: Jesus rose bodily, with scars, eating fish (Luke 24:39–43), and Paul expects ‘the redemption of our body’ (Romans 8:23). And God fills bodies with his Spirit, so that the body is ‘a temple of the Holy Spirit’ (1 Corinthians 6:19).",
      "This stands against an old tendency that Christians have repeatedly had to resist. Gnostic groups in the early centuries taught that matter was evil or inferior and that salvation meant the spirit escaping the body. Some strands of Platonism also saw the body as a prison of the soul. The early church fought these ideas. Irenaeus insisted that the flesh is capable of receiving God’s life, and the creeds confess ‘the resurrection of the body’. Yet popular Christianity has often slipped back into imagining salvation as souls floating away to heaven. The book calls this a loss of the gospel’s earthy hope.",
      "Paul does not ask us to escape our bodies but to offer them: ‘present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable to God’ (Romans 12:1). And he says that the same Spirit who raised Jesus ‘will also give life to your mortal bodies’ (Romans 8:11). Many read that verse as only about the future resurrection. This stream also reads it as describing the Spirit’s life touching our bodies now, as a foretaste of the resurrection.",
      "Healing is central to this vision. The book says that ‘healing is not a perk of salvation, it is the embodiment of it’. Jesus spent a remarkable amount of his ministry healing bodies. Matthew links this directly to Isaiah 53: ‘He healed all who were sick; that it might be fulfilled … He took our infirmities, and bore our diseases’ (Matthew 8:16–17, quoting Isaiah 53:4). The Hebrew words in Isaiah 53:4 can mean sicknesses and pains, though many translations render them ‘griefs’ and ‘sorrows’. In this view, the cross deals with the whole person, not only with guilt. The book also insists that God does not use disease to discipline his children.",
      "The book brings in nervous-system science as part of ‘embodied transformation’. The basic idea is easy to grasp. Our bodies carry our fears. Chronic threat keeps the nervous system on alert; safety allows it to rest and heal. Scripture speaks of peace that guards our hearts and minds (Philippians 4:7), of perfect love casting out fear (1 John 4:18), and of entering God’s rest (Hebrews 4). This stream sees union with Christ as reaching down even into the body’s stress responses. Science here is offered as a description of how bodies work, not as proof of doctrine, and readers should look to the book itself for its specific claims.",
      "A key phrase in the book is a move from ‘faith toward God’ to ‘the faith of God’. In Mark 11:22, Jesus says, in the Greek, <em>echete pistin theou</em>. Most translations render this ‘have faith in God’. But the Greek literally reads ‘have faith of God’, and the KJV margin gives ‘have the faith of God’ as an alternative. Grammatically the phrase can mean faith directed toward God, or God’s own faith and faithfulness shared with us. Most scholars prefer the first; this stream draws on the second. The idea fits the wider ‘faith of Christ’ theme: our trust rests on, and shares in, God’s own faithfulness, rather than straining to produce enough faith.",
      "The book’s vision is that the body is ‘not a souvenir of Adam but a sanctuary for Christ’, and that embodied life should flow from union, not from guilt. It speaks of living as ‘manifest sons and daughters’, echoing Romans 8:19, where creation waits for the revealing of God’s children. This should be distinguished from the ‘Manifest Sons of God’ teaching of the 1940s–50s Latter Rain movement, which made claims about an elite company of perfected believers. The Romans 8 hope is for all God’s children and for creation itself."
    ],
    separation: "The soul is what matters; the body is temporary and will be left behind. Sickness may be God’s way of teaching or disciplining, death is a promotion to heaven, and healing is an occasional bonus rather than part of salvation.",
    union: "God’s plan moves through bodies: forming them, entering one, redeeming them and filling them with his Spirit. The body is a temple and a sanctuary for Christ. Healing expresses salvation, the Spirit gives life to mortal bodies, and embodied life flows from union rather than guilt.",
    voices: [
      { name: "A contemporary New Creation teacher", note: "Lead author of a recent book on embodied New Creation theology (2026)." },
      { name: "Co-authors of a recent book on embodied New Creation theology", note: "Co-authors of the book with its lead author." },
      { name: "Irenaeus of Lyons", note: "Opposed Gnostic contempt for the body, insisting that flesh can receive God’s life and will be raised." },
      { name: "Tertullian", note: "Early Latin writer who wrote <em>On the Resurrection of the Flesh</em> and is known for the phrase that the flesh is the hinge of salvation." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "What about Paul’s thorn in the flesh (2 Corinthians 12:7–10)?", a: "Paul asked three times for it to be removed and was told, ‘My grace is sufficient for you’. Traditional readers often see it as an illness God allowed for Paul’s good. Others note Paul calls it ‘a messenger of Satan’ and that the text never says it was sickness; some think it was persecution. This stream would point out that it is described as from Satan, not from God, and would resist making it a rule that God wants people sick. The honest answer is that we do not know what the thorn was." },
      { q: "Paul told Timothy to take wine for his stomach (1 Timothy 5:23) and left Trophimus sick at Miletus (2 Timothy 4:20). Epaphroditus nearly died (Philippians 2:25–27). Doesn’t this show healing was not automatic even for apostles?", a: "Yes, these texts show that the apostles did not see everyone healed, and that they used ordinary remedies. A healing-focused theology must be honest about that. This stream would say these are real experiences in a world not yet fully renewed, that Paul says God had mercy on Epaphroditus, and that none of these passages say God sent the sickness. Critics reply that they show healing is a gift God gives as he wills, not something guaranteed now." },
      { q: "Doesn’t this teaching risk blaming sick people for lack of faith?", a: "That is a serious danger, and it has done real harm in some healing movements. Telling the sick they simply did not believe enough adds shame to suffering. The move toward ‘the faith of God’ is, in part, a way of answering this, since it rests healing on God’s faithfulness rather than the sick person’s effort. A loving community will pray boldly for healing and also stay with people, without blame, when healing does not come." },
      { q: "Isn’t nervous-system science being used to prop up theology?", a: "It can be if overstated. Research on stress, safety and the body is real, but popular summaries often simplify it. It is best seen as a helpful description of how bodies respond to fear and peace, which fits Scripture’s concern for the whole person, not as scientific proof of a particular theology." }
    ],
    related: ["incarnation", "resurrection", "manifest-sons", "life-immortality", "salvific-ontology", "sacred-secular"]
  },
  {
    id: "salvific-ontology",
    title: "Salvific Ontology",
    tagline: "What defines you: the Fall in Genesis 3, or God’s image in Genesis 1 and your life in Christ?",
    keyVerses: [
      { ref: "Genesis 1:26", text: "God said, ‘Let’s make man in our image, after our likeness.’" },
      { ref: "Ephesians 1:4", text: "Even as he chose us in him before the foundation of the world, that we would be holy and without defect before him in love." },
      { ref: "2 Corinthians 5:14, 17", text: "One died for all, therefore all died … Therefore if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old things have passed away. Behold, all things have become new." },
      { ref: "Colossians 3:3–4", text: "For you died, and your life is hidden with Christ in God. When Christ, our life, is revealed, then you will also be revealed with him in glory." }
    ],
    body: [
      "<em>Ontology</em> is a philosopher’s word for the study of being: what something most deeply is. Every gospel carries an ontology, even if no one names it. When we tell people who they are before God, we are making a claim about their being. A contemporary New Creation teacher has framed the difference between two gospels as a difference between two ontologies, discussed in a podcast conversation (2026).",
      "A <strong>tragic ontology</strong> defines humanity at its root by the Fall. The story of who you are starts in Genesis 3: you are a sinner, separated from God, and fear is the natural shape of your relationship with him. Grace, in this picture, is a rescue operation laid on top of a being that is fundamentally broken.",
      "A <strong>salvific ontology</strong> defines humanity’s true being by Christ. The story of who you are starts in Genesis 1:26, where God makes humanity in his image, and, even before that, in Christ, in whom we were ‘chosen … before the foundation of the world’ (Ephesians 1:4). Your deepest identity is inclusion and belovedness, not separation and fear. In this framing, as this teacher puts it, the gospel is an ‘awakening to who we have always been in Christ’. This teacher has also preached that we are saved <em>into</em> the triune God’s perichoresis, the shared life of Father, Son and Spirit.",
      "Paul gives this framing strong texts. ‘One died for all, therefore all died’ (2 Corinthians 5:14). In Christ there is ‘a new creation’ (5:17). ‘You died, and your life is hidden with Christ in God’ (Colossians 3:3). These are statements about being, not only about legal status. The call of the gospel then sounds like Ephesians 5:14: ‘Awake, you who sleep, and arise from the dead, and Christ will shine on you.’ We are not trying to become something we are not. We are waking up to what God has made true.",
      "This framing echoes very old ideas. <strong>Athanasius</strong>, in <em>On the Incarnation</em>, taught that by taking human nature the Word healed it from the inside, reversing its slide toward corruption and death. Salvation was a change in what humanity is, not only in how God regards it. <strong>Irenaeus</strong> taught <em>recapitulation</em>: Christ, the new Adam, went back over the whole of human life and set it right, summing humanity up in himself. Both describe salvation as <em>theosis</em> or participation in God’s life.",
      "<strong>Maximus the Confessor</strong> in the seventh century taught that every creature has a <em>logos</em>, a word or meaning held in the eternal Logos, Christ. Who you truly are is defined by God’s word for you in Christ, not by your fall from it. In the twentieth century <strong>T. F. Torrance</strong> spoke of an ‘ontological’ view of the atonement: in Christ’s incarnation, life, death and resurrection, God united himself to human being and healed it, so that atonement happens within the very being of Christ, not merely as a legal exchange outside him.",
      "The contrast is with models that are <em>only</em> forensic, that is, legal. In a forensic-only frame, salvation is a change in your legal standing: you are declared not guilty while your being remains what it was. Protestant theology has often held justification as forensic together with union with Christ and regeneration, so this is not a criticism of the courtroom image as such. The concern is with gospels that stop there, leaving people with a new legal status but an old identity rooted in the Fall.",
      "This framing is recent, and no fuller published definition of ‘salvific ontology’ was found beyond his preaching and podcast conversation. What is presented here is a careful explanation of his framing and its historical echoes. Readers who want to understand it in his own words should go to this teacher’s own teaching."
    ],
    separation: "Humanity is defined at its root by the Fall. We are sinners by nature, separated from God and rightly afraid. Salvation changes our legal standing for those who believe, but our basic identity begins in Genesis 3.",
    union: "Humanity is defined by God’s image and by Christ, in whom we were chosen before creation and in whom humanity died and rose. Our truest being is inclusion and belovedness. The gospel calls us to awaken to who we have always been in Christ.",
    voices: [
      { name: "A contemporary New Creation teacher", note: "Pastor and teacher who contrasts a ‘tragic ontology’ with a ‘salvific ontology’; his own teaching is the source to consult for the framing." },
      { name: "Athanasius", note: "Taught in <em>On the Incarnation</em> that the Word healed human nature by taking it, reversing corruption and death." },
      { name: "Irenaeus of Lyons", note: "Taught that Christ ‘recapitulates’ humanity, summing up and restoring the whole of human life in himself." },
      { name: "Maximus the Confessor", note: "Seventh-century theologian who taught that the <em>logoi</em> of all creatures are held in the one Logos, Christ." },
      { name: "T. F. Torrance", note: "Spoke of an ‘ontological’ atonement taking place within the incarnate being of Christ." }
    ],
    objections: [
      { q: "Doesn’t Ephesians 2:1–3 say we were ‘dead in trespasses’ and ‘by nature children of wrath’?", a: "It does, and any honest ontology must reckon with it. Traditional readers see this as our true condition outside Christ until we believe. Relational readers note that Paul immediately says, ‘But God, being rich in mercy … made us alive together with Christ’ (2:4–5), and read the ‘dead’ condition as the tragic state from which God has already acted in Christ. The debate is about whether the Fall or Christ has the final word on human being, and when that word applies." },
      { q: "John 3:18 says whoever does not believe is ‘condemned already’. Doesn’t that support a tragic ontology for unbelievers?", a: "Many read it that way. Relational teachers note that the next verse defines the condemnation: ‘the light has come into the world, and men loved the darkness’ (3:19). They see condemnation as the experience of refusing the light, not God redefining a person’s being. Traditional theologians reply that this still means real separation for those who do not believe. It is a genuine point of disagreement." },
      { q: "Isn’t ‘awakening’ language closer to Eastern religions than to Christianity?", a: "Waking up is biblical language (Ephesians 5:14; Romans 13:11). The difference is that Christian awakening is to Christ, a person who has acted in history, not to an impersonal divine self. Critics are right that the language must stay anchored in the incarnation, cross and resurrection." }
    ],
    related: ["undoing-adam", "incarnation", "sin", "separation-union", "trinitarian-gospel"]
  }
]);

RG.misses = [
  { id: "distant-judge", title: "God as Father, not distant judge", religion: "Religion pictures God as a distant judge watching from above, keeping records and waiting to punish.", gospel: "Jesus reveals God as a Father who runs to embrace his children (Luke 15:20). He came near in the Son, and the Son says that whoever has seen him has seen the Father. Judgment, in his hands, is setting things right in love.", refs: ["Luke 15:20", "John 14:9", "1 John 4:8"] },
  { id: "torn-veil", title: "The veil is torn", religion: "Religion says access to God comes through rituals, holy places and special mediators who stand between you and him.", gospel: "When Jesus died, the temple veil was torn from top to bottom (Matthew 27:51). There is one mediator, Christ Jesus, and through him we come boldly to God. No priestly class or ritual stands in the way.", refs: ["Matt 27:51", "1 Tim 2:5", "Heb 10:19–22"] },
  { id: "new-creation-not-sin-management", title: "New creation, not sin management", religion: "Religion focuses on managing sin: tracking failures, confessing them and trying harder to keep a better record.", gospel: "The gospel announces that in Christ you are a new creation (2 Corinthians 5:17). The old self was crucified with him (Romans 6:6). Freedom from sin grows from a new life, not from endless monitoring of the old one.", refs: ["2 Cor 5:17", "Rom 6:6", "Gal 2:20"] },
  { id: "love-not-fear", title: "Love, not fear, as motivation", religion: "Religion often uses fear of punishment, rejection or hell to motivate obedience.", gospel: "Perfect love casts out fear, because fear has to do with punishment (1 John 4:18). We love because he first loved us. Obedience that grows from love lasts; obedience driven by fear breaks under pressure.", refs: ["1 John 4:18–19", "Rom 8:15", "2 Cor 5:14"] },
  { id: "new-creation-not-escape", title: "New creation, not escape", religion: "Religion often pictures salvation as the soul escaping the body and the earth to go to heaven.", gospel: "The Christian hope is the resurrection of the body and the renewal of all things. Jesus rose bodily, and creation itself will be set free (Romans 8:21). God is making all things new, not abandoning what he made.", refs: ["Rom 8:21–23", "1 Cor 15:42–44", "Rev 21:1–5"] },
  { id: "identity-first", title: "Identity before behaviour", religion: "Religion says: behave correctly and you will become acceptable to God.", gospel: "The gospel says: you are beloved, now live from it. Paul tells us who we are in Christ before telling us how to walk. Behaviour flows from identity, not the other way round.", refs: ["Eph 4:1", "Col 3:1–3", "1 John 3:1–3"] },
  { id: "sabbath-rest", title: "Sabbath as rest in Christ", religion: "Religion turns the Sabbath into a rule to keep, with lists of what may and may not be done.", gospel: "Jesus said the Sabbath was made for people, not people for the Sabbath (Mark 2:27), and invited the weary to come to him for rest. Hebrews speaks of a rest we enter by faith. Sabbath is a gift, fulfilled in Christ.", refs: ["Mark 2:27", "Matt 11:28–30", "Heb 4:9–10", "Col 2:16–17"] },
  { id: "law-as-tutor", title: "The Law as tutor, not ladder", religion: "Religion treats the Law as a ladder to climb toward God by careful obedience.", gospel: "Paul says the Law was a guardian or tutor until Christ came (Galatians 3:24–25). It showed us our need and pointed to him. Now righteousness comes as a gift, and love fulfils what the Law was aiming at.", refs: ["Gal 3:24–25", "Rom 10:4", "Rom 13:10"] },
  { id: "all-included", title: "All included, not insiders and outsiders", religion: "Religion draws sharp lines between insiders and outsiders, the clean and the unclean, the saved and the rest.", gospel: "Jesus ate with outsiders and broke down the dividing wall between peoples (Ephesians 2:14). One died for all, and God was in Christ reconciling the world. The gospel invites people to discover a belonging that is already theirs in him.", refs: ["Eph 2:14–16", "2 Cor 5:14–19", "Luke 15:1–2"] },
  { id: "generosity-not-tax", title: "Generosity, not a tax", religion: "Religion treats the tithe as a tax owed to God, often with threats of curse for those who fail to pay.", gospel: "The New Testament calls for cheerful, generous giving from the heart, not under compulsion (2 Corinthians 9:7). Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the Law. Generosity flows from a Father who gave everything.", refs: ["2 Cor 9:7", "Gal 3:13", "Acts 4:32–35"] },
  { id: "holiness-as-wholeness", title: "Holiness as wholeness and nearness", religion: "Religion understands holiness mainly as separation: avoiding unclean people and places to stay pure.", gospel: "In Jesus, holiness moves toward people. He touched lepers and the leper became clean rather than Jesus becoming unclean. Holiness is God’s wholeness and love that heals what it touches.", refs: ["Mark 1:40–42", "Mark 5:25–34", "1 Thess 5:23"] },
  { id: "prayer-as-communion", title: "Prayer as communion", religion: "Religion treats prayer as persuading a reluctant God, with enough words, fervour or repetition.", gospel: "Jesus said the Father knows what we need before we ask (Matthew 6:8). Prayer is sharing life with a Father who already loves us, joining the Son’s own prayer by the Spirit who prays within us.", refs: ["Matt 6:7–8", "Rom 8:26–27", "Heb 7:25"] },
  { id: "faith-of-christ", title: "Christ’s faith, not our work", religion: "Religion makes faith into another work we must produce in enough quantity to earn God’s response.", gospel: "Our faith rests on the faithfulness of Christ, who trusted and obeyed for us. Some translations of Galatians 2:20 read ‘the faith of the Son of God’. Faith is our response to his faithfulness, not a payment.", refs: ["Gal 2:20", "Heb 12:2", "Eph 2:8–9"] },
  { id: "cross-reconciles-us", title: "The cross reconciles us to God", religion: "Religion often presents the cross as Jesus appeasing an angry Father who could not love us otherwise.", gospel: "Paul says God was in Christ reconciling the world to himself (2 Corinthians 5:19). The cross is God’s own love going to the depths for us, not the Son changing the Father’s mind. Christians debate how to understand the atonement, but the Father and Son act as one.", refs: ["2 Cor 5:18–19", "Rom 5:8", "John 3:16"] },
  { id: "church-as-family", title: "Church as family, not building", religion: "Religion identifies the church with a building, a service or an institution you attend.", gospel: "The church is the household of God and the body of Christ, a people, not a place. Believers are living stones built together, and every member is a priest. Wherever the family gathers in Christ’s name, he is present.", refs: ["Eph 2:19–22", "1 Pet 2:5, 9", "Matt 18:20"] },
  { id: "god-with-us-in-suffering", title: "God with us in suffering", religion: "Religion often says God sends suffering as a teacher or punishment to shape or correct us.", gospel: "Jesus is Immanuel, God with us, who entered our suffering and wept at a friend’s grave. God works good in all things for those who love him (Romans 8:28), but that is not the same as saying he causes the harm. Nothing can separate us from his love.", refs: ["Matt 1:23", "John 11:35", "Rom 8:28, 35–39", "Heb 4:15"] },
  { id: "bible-witness-to-christ", title: "The Bible as witness to Christ", religion: "Religion uses the Bible as a rulebook, flattening every verse into equal commands.", gospel: "Jesus said the Scriptures testify about him (John 5:39). The Bible is one story that finds its centre in Christ, the living Word. We read every page in his light.", refs: ["John 5:39–40", "Luke 24:27", "Heb 1:1–3"] },
  { id: "sacred-ordinary", title: "The sacredness of ordinary life", religion: "Religion separates the spiritual from the ordinary, treating church activities as holy and work, meals and family as secular.", gospel: "In Christ all things hold together, and whatever we do, even eating and drinking, can be done to the glory of God (1 Corinthians 10:31). God became flesh in a carpenter’s workshop. Ordinary life is the place of communion.", refs: ["1 Cor 10:31", "Col 3:17, 23", "Col 1:17"] }
];
